Issue H-6: Incorrect epoch is used in voting weight
calculation, TVL component will be nullified. [RE-
SOLVED]
Source: https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-02-kaskad-feb-16th-2026/issues/36


Summary
_calculateTvlComponent() uses governor.currentEpoch() (epoch N) to compute uptime,
but voting in KaskadGovernor.vote() occurs during epoch N for epoch N-1. This means
voting weight is calculated against the just-started current epoch rather than the
completed epoch being voted on, resulting in artificially suppressed and
time-dependent voting weights.


Vulnerability Detail
When a user calls vote(), the governor determines the voting epoch as current - 1:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-02-kaskad-feb-16th-2026/blob/main/kaskad-
squashed/src/dao/KaskadGovernor.sol#L111-L114

 uint256 current = currentEpoch();
 if (current == 0) revert NoPastEpoch();

 uint256 epoch = current - 1;

Voting only opens after epoch N-1 ends (enforced at line 122: if (block.timestamp < epo
chEnd) revert VotingWindowNotStarted()), so voters are always in epoch N when casting
votes for epoch N-1.
The governor then fetches the user's voting weight via the strategy:
https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-02-kaskad-feb-16th-2026/blob/main/kaskad-
squashed/src/dao/KaskadGovernor.sol#L141-L143

 strategy.syncForVoting(msg.sender);

 uint256 weight = strategy.votingWeight(msg.sender);

Inside _calculateTvlComponent(), the epoch used for uptime calculation is governor.cur
rentEpoch(), which returns epoch N (the current, in-progress epoch):

 function _calculateTvlComponent(
     address account
 ) internal view returns (uint256) {
     uint256 epoch = governor.currentEpoch();

      UserEpochState storage state = userEpochState[account];

     uint256 supplyTerm = _calculateSupplyTerm(state, epoch);
     uint256 borrowTerm = _calculateBorrowTerm(account, state, epoch);

     return (lambdaTvl * (supplyTerm + borrowTerm)) / PRECISION;
 }

Both _calculateSupplyTerm() and _calculateBorrowTerm() pass this epoch to
_calculateUptimeBps(), which computes uptime as the fraction of the epoch the user has
been eligible:

 uint256 effectiveEnd = block.timestamp < epochEnd
     ? block.timestamp
     : epochEnd;

 // ...

 uint256 eligibleTime = effectiveEnd - effectiveStart;
 return uint16((eligibleTime * 10_000) / epochDuration);

Since voting occurs right after epoch N-1 ends (i.e., at the start of epoch N), block.timest
amp is close to epochStart of epoch N, resulting in a near-zero uptime value.
Critically, _calculateSupplyTerm() and _calculateBorrowTerm() enforce minimum uptime
thresholds before returning a non-zero value:

 uint16 public supplierUptimeThresholdBps = 9000; // 90%
 uint16 public borrowerUptimeThresholdBps = 5500; // 55%

At line 250 and line 287:

 if (uptimeBps < supplierUptimeThresholdBps) {
     return 0;
 }

The voting window is 7 days (VOTING_WINDOW = 7 days) and the epoch duration is 30 days
(DURATION = 30 days). Even at the very end of the voting window, the maximum possible
uptime against epoch N is 7 / 30 � 23.3%, far below both the 55% borrower threshold
and the 90% supplier threshold. Both _calculateSupplyTerm() and _calculateBorrowTerm
() will always return 0 during the entire voting window, making the TVL component
permanently zero for all voters.


Impact
The TVL component of voting weight is completely nullified for all users across the entire
voting window. Since the maximum achievable uptime against the current epoch

 (�23.3%) can never meet the minimum thresholds (55%/90%), both supply and borrow
terms always return 0. This means:
     • Voting weight is reduced to only the loyalty term (staking + activity), entirely
       removing the TVL-based governance influence that the protocol explicitly
       designed.
     • Users with large supply/borrow positions but modest staking positions lose their
       intended governance power.
     • If the loyalty term is also zero for a user (e.g., no stKSKD staked), the vote() call
       reverts with ZeroWeight() at line 146, completely preventing them from voting.


Recommendation
Use the previous (completed) epoch for uptime calculation within _calculateTvlComponen
t(), aligning the uptime measurement with the epoch being voted on:

 function _calculateTvlComponent(
     address account
 ) internal view returns (uint256) {
 -   uint256 epoch = governor.currentEpoch();
 +   uint256 currentEp = governor.currentEpoch();
 +   uint256 epoch = currentEp > 0 ? currentEp - 1 : 0;
     UserEpochState storage state = userEpochState[account];

      uint256 supplyTerm = _calculateSupplyTerm(state, epoch);
      uint256 borrowTerm = _calculateBorrowTerm(account, state, epoch);

      return (lambdaTvl * (supplyTerm + borrowTerm)) / PRECISION;
 }



