Issue H-8: Recycled amount is not tracked accurately,
partial amount will be stuck in the contract. [RE-
SOLVED]
Source: https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2026-02-kaskad-feb-16th-2026/issues/38


Summary
_autoRecycleEpoch is triggered by the first claim after an epoch ends, but runs before
users' epochs are closed via closeUserEpochs. This causes undistributed rewards
recorded after recycling to be permanently stuck in the contract, never burned and
never forwarded to the next epoch.


Vulnerability Detail
When a user claims rewards, the flow in KaskadRewardsController.claimRewards()
executes in this order:
 function claimRewards(...) external override whenNotPaused returns (uint256) {
     if (to == address(0)) revert InvalidToAddress();
     _maybeDistributePreviousEpoch(); // 1. Recycles previous epoch
     if (address(activityTracker) != address(0)) {
         activityTracker.closeUserEpochs(assets, msg.sender); // 2. Closes user's
          ,→ past epochs
     }
     return _claimRewards(assets, amount, msg.sender, msg.sender, to, reward);
 }

Step 1 calls _maybeDistributePreviousEpoch() which calls
_autoRecycleEpoch(prevEpoch):

 function _autoRecycleEpoch(uint256 epoch) internal {
     address reward = address(KSKD_TOKEN);

     // Skip if already recycled or nothing to recycle
     if (epochRecycled[epoch][reward]) return; // @audit returns on all subsequent
      ,→ calls

     uint256 undistributed = undistributedPerEpoch[epoch][reward];
     if (undistributed == 0) {
         epochRecycled[epoch][reward] = true;
         return;
     }

     epochRecycled[epoch][reward] = true; // @audit locks recycling permanently

        uint16 toNextEpochBps = STRATEGY.undistributedToNextEpochBps();
       uint256 toNextEpoch = (undistributed * toNextEpochBps) / 10000;
       uint256 toBurn = undistributed - toNextEpoch;

       if (toNextEpoch > 0) {
           recycledForEpoch[epoch + 1][reward] += toNextEpoch;
       }
       if (toBurn > 0) {
           KSKD_TOKEN.burn(toBurn);
       }
       // ...
 }

Step 2 then calls closeUserEpochs, which processes the claiming user's past epochs. For
ineligible users, this calls recordUndistributed() — adding to undistributedPerEpoch[prevE
poch] after recycling has already completed and locked the epoch.
The issue is compounded by the fact that closeUserEpochs is only triggered per-user
when they personally claim or interact with the protocol. There is no keeper or batch
mechanism to close all users' epochs before recycling occurs. This means:
     1. First claimer in epoch N triggers _autoRecycleEpoch(N-1) which reads whatever
        partial undistributedPerEpoch[N-1] exists and sets epochRecycled = true. Then
        their own closeUserEpochs may add more undistributed to epoch N-1.
     2. All subsequent claimers hit the if (epochRecycled[epoch][reward]) return guard
        at line 147 and skip recycling. Their closeUserEpochs continues adding to undistribu
        tedPerEpoch[N-1].
     3. The permissionless recycleUndistributed() cannot help — it reverts with EpochAlready
        Recycled since the flag is already set.
All undistributed amounts recorded after the first claim's auto-recycle are trapped in the
contract.


Impact
     • KSKD tokens permanently stuck: Undistributed rewards that should have been
       burned or recycled to the next epoch remain locked in the contract.
     • Understated recycledForEpoch: The next epoch receives fewer recycled tokens
       than intended, reducing total rewards available for distribution.
     • Magnitude scales with user inactivity: The more users that claim lazily (i.e., don't
       interact until well after the epoch ends), the larger the amount of tokens trapped.
       In a protocol where many users may only claim periodically, this could represent a
       significant portion of undistributed rewards each epoch.

 Recommendation
We need a re-design of how the recycling mechanism works. Simplest would be to pool
recycled amounts into the vault whenever they are generated and then distribute them
in the next epoch. This means that undistributed tokens from epoch X could be given to
X + N and not necessarily N+1.


Discussion
KaiReytsu
Fixed. Fix details in https://github.com/Kaskad-Lending/kaskad-squashed/pull/8

[H-1] Contracts interacting with `PositionRouter` may lose funds

  Severity: High                               Status: Resolved

Context:

      BasePositionManager.sol#L285

Description:

The LiqMarket protocol uses _transferOutETHWithGasLimitIgnoreFail function to send
ETH to recipients:


       function _transferOutETHWithGasLimitIgnoreFail(uint256 _amountOut,
   address payable _receiver) internal {
               IWETH(weth).withdraw(_amountOut);

           // use `send` instead of `transfer` to not revert whole
   transaction in case ETH transfer was failed
           // it has limit of 2300 gas
           // this is to avoid front-running
   >>      _receiver.send(_amountOut);
       }


The send function uses only 2300 gas to transfer ETH, which may not be sufficient if the
recipient is a contract wallet with custom logic in its receive function. Some Gnosis Safe
wallets might require more than 2300 gas for a transaction to succeed. Since there is no
check to verify whether the send was successful, the withdrawn ETH could become trapped
in the router, causing the receiver to lose their funds.
For example, in a scenario where executeDecreasePositions is called and
request.withdrawETH is set to true, the withdrawn ETH will remain in the router.

Recommendation:

 It is recommended to use call with a specified gasLimit to transfer ETH, and fallback to
transferring WETH if the ETH transfer fails, similar to GMX:


       function _transferOutETHWithGasLimitFallbackToWeth(uint256
   _amountOut, address payable _receiver) internal {
           IWETH _weth = IWETH(weth);
           _weth.withdraw(_amountOut);

              // re-assign ethTransferGasLimit since only local variables
              // can be used in assembly calls
              uint256 _ethTransferGasLimit = ethTransferGasLimit;

              bool success;
              // use an assembly call to avoid loading large data into memory
              // input mem[in…(in+insize)]
              // output area mem[out…(out+outsize))]
              assembly {
                  success := call(
                      _ethTransferGasLimit, // gas limit
                      _receiver, // receiver
                      _amountOut, // value
                      0, // in
                      0, // insize
                      0, // out
                      0 // outsize
                  )
              }

              if (success) { return; }

              // if the transfer failed, re-wrap the token and send it to the
   receiver
              _weth.deposit{ value: _amountOut }();
              _weth.transfer(address(_receiver), _amountOut);
        }


As a compromise it is possible to keep send and transfer WETH in case of failure:


       function _transferOutETHWithGasLimitIgnoreFail(uint256 _amountOut,
   address payable _receiver) internal {
           IWETH(weth).withdraw(_amountOut);

           // use `send` instead of `transfer` to not revert whole
   transaction in case ETH transfer was failed

                // it has limit of 2300 gas
               // this is to avoid front-running
   +           bool success = _receiver.send(_amountOut);
   +           if(!success) {
   +             _weth.deposit{ value: _amountOut }();
   +             _weth.transfer(address(_receiver), _amountOut);
   +           }
           }


